L["last-supper"] = """It was the first day of the Festival of Unleavened Bread, when the Passover lamb had to be sacrificed. Jesus sent Peter and John ahead to prepare the meal. They found an upper room, large and furnished, just as he had told them. That evening, Jesus reclined at the table with his twelve disciples.

He said to them, I have eagerly desired to eat this Passover with you before I suffer. Passover was the meal that remembered how God rescued Israel from slavery in Egypt, when the blood of a lamb marked the doors and the people were spared. Jesus was about to give that old meal a new meaning.

During the meal, Jesus got up from the table, took off his outer clothing, and wrapped a towel around his waist. He poured water into a basin and began to wash his disciples' feet. This was the job of the lowest servant. Peter objected at first. Lord, are you going to wash my feet? Jesus told him that unless he washed him, Peter had no part with him. When he was done, he said, I have set you an example that you should do as I have done for you.

Then he took bread, gave thanks, broke it, and gave it to them, saying, this is my body given for you. Do this in remembrance of me. In the same way, after the supper he took the cup, saying, this cup is the new covenant in my blood, which is poured out for you. He was telling them that his death was coming, and that it would be the new way for people to be forgiven and brought to God. He also told them that one of them would betray him. They were deeply distressed and each asked, surely you don't mean me, Lord?

He gave them a new command. A new command I give you. Love one another. As I have loved you, so you must love one another. By this everyone will know that you are my disciples, if you love one another. He told them not to let their hearts be troubled. In my Father's house are many rooms. I am going there to prepare a place for you. And he promised to send the Holy Spirit to be with them. He said, I am the way and the truth and the life. No one comes to the Father except through me.

Before they went out, they sang a hymn.

Why does this matter? This meal is the root of Communion, which Christians have shared ever since. Jesus took the bread and cup of an old story and showed how he would fulfill it. He is the Lamb who saves his people. And it shows his heart in what he did first, which was to wash their feet.

What happened after? After supper, he led them out to the Garden of Gethsemane, and the events of Good Friday began. The disciples scattered, but they would gather again after the resurrection, and they continued to break bread together.

What does this mean for us today? Each time we share Communion, we remember what he did. It is also a call to serve. The greatest person in the room knelt to wash feet, and he asks us to do the same. And it gives us a new commandment that people will recognize his followers by, which is love.

That is why this story is so important. It says that Jesus gave himself willingly for us, and that he left us a meal to remember him by until he returns."""

L["gethsemane"] = """After the Last Supper, Jesus went out with his disciples across the Kidron Valley to a garden on the Mount of Olives called Gethsemane, which means olive press. It was a place he often went with them. When he arrived, he said to his disciples, sit here while I go over there and pray.

He took Peter, James and John along with him, and he began to be deeply distressed and troubled. My soul is overwhelmed with sorrow to the point of death, he said to them. Stay here and keep watch with me. Notice that Jesus asked for company. He didn't want to be alone.

Going a little farther, he fell with his face to the ground and prayed, my Father, if it is possible, may this cup be taken from me. Yet not as I will, but as you will. Jesus knew what was coming the next day. The cup stood for the suffering and the judgment that he was about to take. He was honest with his Father about how hard it was. And he surrendered.

Then he returned to his disciples and found them sleeping. Could you men not keep watch with me for one hour? he asked Peter. Watch and pray so that you will not fall into temptation. The spirit is willing, but the flesh is weak. He went away a second time and prayed the same thing. Again he came back and found them sleeping, because their eyes were heavy. So he left them and went away once more and prayed the third time, saying the same thing.

Luke, who was a doctor, adds that an angel from heaven appeared to him and strengthened him. And being in anguish, he prayed more earnestly, and his sweat was like drops of blood falling to the ground.

Then he returned to the disciples and said to them, are you still sleeping and resting? Look, the hour has come. Rise, let us go. Here comes my betrayer. He was calm. The struggle was over, and he was ready.

Why does this matter? Gethsemane shows the full humanity of Jesus. He wasn't pretending. He felt sorrow and dread. And it shows his obedience. The first Adam, in a garden, chose his own will. Jesus, in a garden, chose the Father's will. The result was the road to the cross.

What happened after? Within minutes, Judas arrived with soldiers, and Jesus was arrested. The disciples fled. But the prayer in the garden had been answered in a way. The cup was not taken away, but he was given strength to drink it.

What does this mean for us today? Jesus teaches us how to pray when things are hard. We can tell God exactly how we feel and ask for what we want, and then say, not my will but yours. It also shows that he understands. When we face something we dread, we pray to someone who has been in that garden. And it reminds us that the people who love us can fail us when we are most in need. But Jesus never fails.

That is why this story is so important. It says that Jesus willingly accepted the Father's will to save us. And it gives us a pattern for praying through our own hardest nights."""
